\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth,height=3.5cm,keepaspectratio]{fig6_dissociation.pdf}
\caption{Detection--decision dissociation across three architectures. For Llama, the linear probe peak (L28, 88\% depth), MLP probe peak (L18, 56\%), and causal steering node (L13, 41\%) occupy distinct layers, revealing a three-way dissociation. Qwen and Gemma achieve comparable probe accuracy but show no significant causal site under linear steering, illustrating that detectability does not imply causal relevance.}
\label{fig:dissociation}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Causal influence is localized to L13 regardless of detection peak.}
In Llama, causal influence is localized to L13 (41\% depth; Fisher $p = 0.002$), regardless of detection peak location (EN$\to$EN at L28, ZH$\to$ZH at L13), with all late-layer interventions producing no significant change ($p > 0.3$; Figure~\ref{fig:dissociation}).
An MLP probe reveals a third dissociation: non-linear conflict features peak at L18 (56\% depth), between the linear peak (L28) and the causal node (L13).
L13 coincides with cross-lingual alignment sites \citep{muller2021first, wendler2024llamas}, suggesting mid-layers serve a dual role in alignment and conflict resolution.
Steering failed in Qwen (L14, $n = 131$, $p = 0.785$) and Gemma (L17, $n = 171$, $p = 0.448$) with zero directional flips; Gemma's 88.9\% probe accuracy rules out probe quality as a confound.

\paragraph{Why does steering fail in Qwen and Gemma?}
Three hypotheses merit consideration: (1)~non-linear causal interactions that single-direction perturbation cannot address \citep{elhage2022toy}; (2)~superposition with other features such that perturbing one direction disrupts unrelated computations \citep{templeton2024scaling}; (3)~architectural differences (e.g., Gemma's sandwich normalization) altering perturbation propagation.

\paragraph{Preliminary hypothesis validation.}
Existing analyses address all three hypotheses.
For~(1), MLP probes gain $+9.2$~pp over linear probes on Llama conflict EN$\to$ZH but $-0.5$~pp on XNLI; Qwen shows a similar pattern ($+2.4$ vs.\ $-2.0$~pp), indicating conflict-specific non-linear encoding.
For~(2), conflict and XNLI probe weight vectors are near-orthogonal (cosine similarity 0.02--0.05), yet cross-task language identity transfer is near-perfect (87--100\%), consistent with shared subspace but distinct discriminative directions, a hallmark of superposition.
For~(3), Qwen and Gemma show mid-layer cross-task transfer dips (L14--L24) absent in Llama; L13 falls in Llama's dip-free region.
Definitive validation requires activation patching \citep{meng2022locating}.

\paragraph{Conflict signals encoded in a general cross-lingual space.}
The ZH$\to$EN transfer advantage ($+7.3$, $+7.2$, $+6.6$~pp) matches the XNLI baseline ($+6.0$, $+6.7$, $+7.8$~pp); this asymmetry piggybacks on general cross-lingual alignment \citep{zhao2026when, kargaran2024mexa, wendler2024llamas}.

\paragraph{Relation to interpretability without actionability.}
\citet{basu2026interpretability} reported steering failure despite 98.2\% AUROC; our L13 succeeds with a weaker signal because we steer at mid-layer rather than probe-peak, while our three-model design isolates architecture.

